---
intent: >
  Terminal error output must match the spec's documented examples exactly —
  short relative path in the message, and an "Error: " prefix on stderr —
  so the developer sees what the spec promised when something goes wrong.
misalignment:
  - implementation-drift
  - test-gap
---

## Issues

- [x] `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts:39` — Error message uses the full
  absolute path (`mcpPath`) rather than the short form `.mcp.json`. The spec's
  good example shows `Error: .mcp.json exists but contains invalid JSON. Fix
  the syntax error and re-run.` — the file reference is the short relative
  name, not an absolute filesystem path.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[1]` — "Silently succeeding while writing
  malformed JSON" (the error message is the user's only signal; if it is
  harder to read than the spec documented, the guard is weaker than intended)
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/init/index.ts:41` — The IIFE catch block writes the raw error
  message to stderr without an "Error: " prefix. The spec's good example shows
  the output as `Error: .mcp.json exists but contains invalid JSON…` — the
  `Error: ` label is part of the documented terminal contract.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[1]` — spec-documented terminal output for
  the malformed-JSON failure case includes the prefix; the implementation omits
  it.
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.test.ts:104-108` — The malformed-JSON
  test asserts only that the thrown error message contains `mcpPath` (the
  absolute temp path). Once the implementation is fixed to use the short
  relative form, this assertion must be updated to match — otherwise it will
  pass against the broken form and fail against the correct one.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[1]` — test must guard the exact error
  contract the spec documents, not the accidental absolute-path form.
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Retro

Both issues share a root cause: the spec's good/bad examples section documents
the exact terminal strings the CLI must produce, but those strings were not
mechanically transferred into the test assertions. The malformed-JSON test
checked that the error contains the file path — a structural check — rather
than asserting the full message shape against the spec's example. A test that
pins the complete error string (including the `Error: ` prefix and the short
relative filename) would have caught both deviations at the time they were
written.

Strengthening the plan stage: the plan's step 5 (test list) named "throws an
error mentioning the file path" as the criterion, which is weaker than "throws
an error whose message matches the spec's documented example verbatim." When a
spec provides exact output strings in its good/bad examples, the plan should
quote them as the acceptance criterion, not paraphrase them.
